/** @mainpage Qore zip Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section zipintro Introduction

    The \c zip module provides comprehensive ZIP archive functionality for Qore, including:

    - Creating, reading, and modifying ZIP archives
    - Multiple compression methods: deflate, bzip2, lzma, zstd, xz
    - ZIP64 support for large files (>4GB) and many entries (>65,535)
    - Traditional PKWARE (ZipCrypto) encryption by default for maximum interoperability
    - Optional AES encryption (128/192/256-bit) for strong security
    - Streaming API for large file operations
    - Data provider module for integration with Qore's data provider framework

    To use this module, use \c "%requires zip" in your code.

    @section zipquickstart Quick Start

    @subsection zipcreate Creating a ZIP Archive

    @code{.py}
#!/usr/bin/qore

%requires zip

# Create a new archive
ZipFile zip("archive.zip", "w");
zip.addText("readme.txt", "Hello, World!");
zip.addFile("document.pdf", "/path/to/document.pdf");
zip.addDirectory("images/");
zip.addFile("images/logo.png", "/path/to/logo.png", {"compression_level": ZIP_COMPRESSION_BEST});
zip.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection zipread Reading a ZIP Archive

    @code{.py}
ZipFile zip("archive.zip", "r");
foreach hash<Qore::Zip::ZipEntryInfo> entry in (zip.entries()) {
    printf("Entry: %s, Size: %d bytes\n", entry.name, entry.size);
}
binary data = zip.read("readme.txt");
zip.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection zipextract Extracting a ZIP Archive

    @code{.py}
ZipFile zip("archive.zip", "r");
zip.extractAll("/destination/path", {"overwrite": True});
zip.close();
    @endcode

    @subsection zipmemory In-Memory Archives

    @code{.py}
# Create an in-memory archive
ZipFile zip();
zip.addText("data.json", make_json({"key": "value"}));
binary archive_data = zip.toData();
# archive_data can be sent over network, stored in DB, etc.

# Read from binary data
ZipFile readZip(archive_data);
string content = readZip.readText("data.json");
    @endcode

    @subsection zipencrypt Password-Protected Archives

    @code{.py}
# Create an encrypted archive. The default encryption method when a password is supplied is
# traditional PKWARE (ZipCrypto), which every common ZIP tool can open out of the box. Use
# ZIP_EM_AES_256 when the data is sensitive and you control which tool the recipient will use.
ZipFile zip("secure.zip", "w");
zip.addText("secret.txt", "Confidential data", NOTHING, {"password": "secret123"});  # PKWARE
zip.addText("topsecret.txt", "Very sensitive", NOTHING,
    {"password": "secret123", "encryption_method": ZIP_EM_AES_256});                   # AES-256
zip.close();
    @endcode

    @section zipcompression Compression Methods

    The module supports multiple compression methods:

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_CM_STORE|No compression (store only)
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_CM_DEFLATE|Standard deflate compression (most compatible)
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_CM_BZIP2|BZip2 compression
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_CM_LZMA|LZMA compression
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_CM_ZSTD|Zstandard compression (fast, high ratio)
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_CM_XZ|XZ/LZMA2 compression

    Compression levels range from 0 (store) to 9 (maximum):

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_COMPRESSION_DEFAULT|Default compression level
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_COMPRESSION_STORE|No compression (level 0)
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_COMPRESSION_FASTEST|Fastest compression (level 1)
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_COMPRESSION_BEST|Best compression (level 9)

    @section zipencryption Encryption

    The module supports multiple encryption methods:

    |!Constant|!Description
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_EM_NONE|No encryption (disables encryption even when a password is set)
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_EM_TRAD_PKWARE|Traditional PKWARE (ZipCrypto) — default; readable by every common ZIP tool
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_EM_AES_128|AES-128 encryption
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_EM_AES_192|AES-192 encryption
    |@ref Qore::Zip::ZIP_EM_AES_256|AES-256 encryption (strongest)

    When a password is provided in @ref Qore::Zip::ZipAddOptions without an explicit
    \c encryption_method, the module uses traditional PKWARE (ZipCrypto) encryption. This is the
    format Windows Explorer's built-in ZIP handler, macOS Finder, and most common tools open
    without any extra software. PKWARE is cryptographically weak and vulnerable to known-plaintext
    attacks; choose one of the AES methods explicitly when the data is sensitive and the recipient
    has a modern ZIP tool (7-Zip, WinRAR, Info-ZIP 6.1+, etc.).

    Extracting an encrypted entry without supplying a password raises \c ZIP-PASSWORD-ERROR
    before any destination file is created, and extracting with an incorrect password raises
    \c ZIP-PASSWORD-ERROR with a "wrong password" message; no 0-byte garbage is left on disk.

    @section zipdataprovider Data Provider API

    The \c ZipDataProvider module provides integration with Qore's data provider framework:
    @htmlonly <a href="../../ZipDataProvider/html/index.html">ZipDataProvider module documentation</a> @endhtmlonly

    @code{.py}
%requires ZipDataProvider

# Get the data provider factory
AbstractDataProviderFactory factory = DataProvider::getFactoryEx("zip");
AbstractDataProvider provider = factory.create();

# Navigate to the create-archive action
AbstractDataProvider createAction = provider.getChildProviderEx("archive").getChildProviderEx("create");

# Execute the action
hash<auto> result = createAction.doRequest({
    "path": "output.zip",
    "files": (
        {"name": "file.txt", "data": "Hello, World!"},
    ),
});

# Or use the shorthand path navigation
AbstractDataProvider dp = DataProvider::getFactoryObjectFromStringEx("zip{}/archive/create");
result = dp.doRequest({
    "path": "output.zip",
    "files": (
        {"name": "file.txt", "data": "Hello, World!"},
    ),
});
    @endcode

    @section ziperrors Error Handling

    ZIP operations throw exceptions of type \c "ZIP-ERROR" for generic failures,
    \c "ZIP-PASSWORD-ERROR" when an encrypted entry needs a password or a supplied password is
    wrong, and \c "ZIP-SECURITY-ERROR" for path-traversal attempts or unsafe symlink targets:

    @code{.py}
try {
    ZipFile zip("archive.zip", "r");
    zip.extractAll("/dest", {"password": "maybe-wrong"});
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    switch (ex.err) {
        case "ZIP-PASSWORD-ERROR":
            printf("Password problem: %s\n", ex.desc);
            break;
        case "ZIP-SECURITY-ERROR":
            printf("Archive is unsafe: %s\n", ex.desc);
            break;
        default:
            printf("ZIP error: %s\n", ex.desc);
    }
}
    @endcode

    @section zipreleasenotes Release Notes

    @subsection zip_1_0 zip Module Version 1.0
    - Initial release
    - Full ZIP64 support
    - Multiple compression methods (deflate, bzip2, lzma, zstd, xz)
    - Encryption: traditional PKWARE (ZipCrypto) by default for maximum interoperability with
      Windows Explorer, macOS Finder, and other common ZIP tools; optional AES-128/192/256 for
      strong encryption
    - \c ZIP-PASSWORD-ERROR raised cleanly when a password is required or incorrect, with no
      0-byte garbage left on disk
    - @ref Qore::Zip::ZipFile::extractAll() returns a @ref Qore::Zip::ZipExtractResult hash
      describing which entries were written and which were skipped because the destination
      already existed
    - @ref Qore::Zip::ZipFile::recompress() accepts an optional \c encryption_method
    - In-memory archive operations
    - Streaming API (@ref Qore::Zip::ZipInputStream / @ref Qore::Zip::ZipOutputStream)
    - Data provider module
    - @c qzip CLI with 11 commands and an \c -e / \c --encryption flag

